  "body": "This change finally get us to `<!DOCTYPE html>` being the only conforming doctype in text/html—by making `<!doctype html SYSTEM \"about:legacy-compat\">` non-conforming if used in text/html (but not in XML).\r\n\r\nWe added `<!DOCTYPE html SYSTEM \"about:legacy-compat\">` many years ago only due to lobbying from advocates for the “polyglot” author-with-XML-tools-but-serve-as-text/html approach, and the only use case cited for it then was the corner case that HTML serializers in existing XSLT engines could not emit just `<!DOCTYPE html>` but instead as a workaround needed to emit a system ID of some kind, and that some XSLT engines such as libxslt were unlikely to ever update their HTML serializers to emit just `<!DOCTYPE html>`.\r\n\r\nBut people quit actively advocating the “polyglot” authoring approach years ago now, and [the polyglot spec has not been updated or touched in more than three years](https://dev.w3.org/cvsweb/html5/html-polyglot/) and was some time ago taken off the W3C Rec track and moved to being just a frozen Note with a giant warning at the top.\r\n\r\nSo it’s time to quit burdening all HTML authors with this corner-case complication to the definition of what a conforming doctype is, and time to quit keeping around this artifact of an authoring approach that no longer even has any other current specification behind it.\r\n\r\n(In hindsight, this change rightly should have been part of #2048. Sorry but I had not thought it all the way through yet til today.)",
